1. A method of manufacturing a printed circuit board, the method comprising:

providing a circuit board substrate having a surface comprising at least one of copper, a dielectric material, glass fiber-reinforced resin, solder mask material, or an insulating laminate;

depositing, by inkjet printing, a patterned layer of a UV-curable inkjet ink onto the surface of the circuit board substrate, the patterned layer defining at least one printed feature of the printed circuit board;

wherein the UV-curable inkjet ink comprises:

a radiation-curable resin system comprising one or more acrylate, methacrylate, epoxy, vinyl ether, or hybrid curable monomers or oligomers;

a photoinitiator configured to initiate curing upon exposure to ultraviolet radiation; and

a silane adhesion promoter present in an amount effective to promote adhesion of the patterned layer to the surface of the circuit board substrate;

exposing the patterned layer to ultraviolet radiation to cure the UV-curable inkjet ink and form a cured ink layer adhered to the surface; and

performing at least one subsequent printed circuit board manufacturing operation on the circuit board substrate bearing the cured ink layer, thereby forming the printed circuit board.

1. A process for forming an inkjet-printed image on a printed circuit board, the process comprising:

   (a) providing a printed circuit board substrate;

   (b) inkjet-printing onto the substrate a radiation-curable inkjet ink comprising at least one polymerisable compound, at least one photoinitiator and a silane additive of formula (I):

   \[
   (R_1)(R_2)(R_3)Si-L-R_4
   \]

   wherein:

   - **L** is a divalent linking group selected from a C1-C12 alkylene group, a C2-C12 alkenylene group, a C6-C12 arylene group, or a C7-C20 aralkylene group, each optionally interrupted by one or more oxygen, sulfur, carbonyl, ester, urethane or amide groups;

   - **R1, R2 and R3** are each independently selected from a hydrolysable group and a C1-C6 alkyl group, provided that at least one of R1, R2 and R3 is a hydrolysable group;

   - the hydrolysable group is selected from C1-C6 alkoxy, aryloxy, acyloxy, oxime, amino, amido and halogen;

   - **R4** is an organic functional group capable of reacting with or interacting with the printed circuit board substrate or with the cured ink, selected from an epoxy group, amino group, mercapto group, acrylate group, methacrylate group, vinyl group, isocyanate group, blocked isocyanate group, ureido group or anhydride group;

   (c) exposing the printed ink to actinic radiation to at least partially cure the ink and fix the printed image on the substrate; and

   (d) subsequently thermally curing the printed ink at a temperature of from 80°C to 200°C to complete curing and promote adhesion of the ink to the printed circuit board substrate.
